## Introduction

I created this article to document the difference between an **existing (stable) deployment** and a **canary deployment** in Kubernetes, and how to test the two side by side. <br>

The idea behind a canary deployment is simple: instead of rolling out a new version to 100% of your users straight away, you release it to a small subset first. If it behaves, you slowly increase its share of traffic. If it misbehaves, you cut it off before it affects everyone.

To keep this easy to reason about, I am following the common convention of naming the stable deployment <code style="color : red">v1</code> and the new one <code style="color : red">v2</code>. To make the difference obvious when testing, <code style="color : red">v1</code> will respond with <code style="color : red">blue</code> and <code style="color : red">v2</code> will respond with <code style="color : red">green</code>. 🔵🟢

There is no service mesh (Istio, Linkerd, etc.) or Ingress controller involved here. This is a "poor man's canary", using plain <code style="color : red">Deployment</code> and <code style="color : red">Service</code> objects, where the canary weight is controlled purely by the **replica count ratio** between the two deployments. This fits well on a small self-hosted cluster like mine, without needing extra add-ons.

## Pre-requisite

The following tools, aside from a running Kubernetes cluster, must be installed on the machine running the commands. Normally this will be the master or control node.
If you are using a jumpbox, then they are installed on the jumpbox itself.

+ Go (1.21+)
+ Docker

> The steps below assume _root_ user, and a cluster similar to my own 3-node setup (1 control node, 2 worker nodes, RHEL VMs on VMware Fusion).

## Step 1: Create source code files

+ Create a dedicated directory, for example: <code style="color : red">/colorapp</code>.

  ```
  mkdir -p /colorapp
  cd /colorapp
  ```

+ Create a file named _main.go_ with below content. This is a tiny Go HTTP server that just returns whatever color it was told to be, via the <code style="color : red">COLOR</code> environment variable.

  ```go
  package main

  import (
    "fmt"
    "log"
    "net/http"
    "os"
  )

  func main() {
    color := os.Getenv("COLOR")
    if color == "" {
      color = "unknown"
    }

    version := os.Getenv("VERSION")
    if version == "" {
      version = "unknown"
    }

    http.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
      fmt.Fprintf(w, "%s\n", color)
      log.Printf("served request, version=%s color=%s", version, color)
    })

    log.Println("🟢 colorapp listening on :8080")
    log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil))
  }
  ```

+ Create a file named _go.mod_.

  ```
  module colorapp

  go 1.21
  ```

+ Create a _Dockerfile_. Same image will be reused for both <code style="color : red">v1</code> and <code style="color : red">v2</code>; only the environment variables differ at deploy time.

  ```
  FROM golang:1.21-alpine AS build
  WORKDIR /app
  COPY go.mod .
  COPY main.go .
  RUN go build -o colorapp .

  FROM alpine:3.19
  COPY --from=build /app/colorapp /usr/local/bin/colorapp
  EXPOSE 8080
  CMD ["colorapp"]
  ```

## Step 2: Build and push the image

+ Build the image and push it to your Docker Hub repository. 🐳 One image is enough, since color and version are injected via environment variables in the Deployment manifests, not baked into the image.

  ```
  # Login to Docker Hub
  docker login

  # Build the image
  docker build -t faizyakob/colorapp:1.0 .

  # Push the image
  docker push faizyakob/colorapp:1.0
  ```

## Step 3: Deploy the existing (stable) version, v1

+ Create a file named _v1-deployment.yaml_. This is our existing, stable deployment. It carries the shared label <code style="color : red">app: colorapp</code>, plus a <code style="color : red">version: v1</code> label to identify it individually.

  ```yaml
  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: Deployment
  metadata:
    name: colorapp-v1
    labels:
      app: colorapp
      version: v1
  spec:
    replicas: 4
    selector:
      matchLabels:
        app: colorapp
        version: v1
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          app: colorapp
          version: v1
      spec:
        containers:
        - name: colorapp
          image: faizyakob/colorapp:1.0
          ports:
          - containerPort: 8080
          env:
          - name: COLOR
            value: "blue"
          - name: VERSION
            value: "v1"
  ```

+ Create a file named _service.yaml_. Note the selector only matches on <code style="color : red">app: colorapp</code>, **not** on <code style="color : red">version</code>. This is what lets the Service load balance across both <code style="color : red">v1</code> and <code style="color : red">v2</code> pods at once.

  ```yaml
  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Service
  metadata:
    name: colorapp
  spec:
    type: NodePort
    selector:
      app: colorapp
    ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
      nodePort: 30090
  ```

+ Apply both manifests.

  ```
  kubectl apply -f v1-deployment.yaml
  kubectl apply -f service.yaml
  ```

+ Verify the stable deployment is up.

  ```
  kubectl get deploy colorapp-v1
  kubectl get pods -l app=colorapp
  kubectl get svc colorapp
  ```

At this point, 100% of traffic is going to <code style="color : red">v1</code>, since it's the only deployment matching the Service's selector.

## Step 4: Deploy the canary version, v2

+ Create a file named _v2-deployment.yaml_. Same shape as before, but labeled <code style="color : red">version: v2</code>, and returning <code style="color : red">green</code> instead of <code style="color : red">blue</code>.

  ```yaml
  apiVersion: apps/v1
  kind: Deployment
  metadata:
    name: colorapp-v2
    labels:
      app: colorapp
      version: v2
  spec:
    replicas: 1
    selector:
      matchLabels:
        app: colorapp
        version: v2
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          app: colorapp
          version: v2
      spec:
        containers:
        - name: colorapp
          image: faizyakob/colorapp:1.0
          ports:
          - containerPort: 8080
          env:
          - name: COLOR
            value: "green"
          - name: VERSION
            value: "v2"
  ```

  Notice <code style="color : red">v1</code> has 4 replicas and <code style="color : red">v2</code> has 1. Since the Service treats every <code style="color : red">colorapp</code> pod equally, this gives us roughly an 80/20 split, 80% stable, 20% canary.

+ Apply the canary deployment.

  ```
  kubectl apply -f v2-deployment.yaml
  ```

+ Confirm both deployments are now running side by side under the same Service.

  ```
  kubectl get deploy -l app=colorapp
  kubectl get pods -l app=colorapp -L version
  ```

## Step 5: Test using curl

+ 🚀 Hit the Service repeatedly via its NodePort, and watch the response flip between <code style="color : red">blue</code> and <code style="color : red">green</code>.

  ```
  for i in $(seq 1 20); do curl -s http://localhost:30090; done
  ```

  Expected output, roughly 80% <code style="color : red">blue</code> and 20% <code style="color : red">green</code>:

  ```
  blue
  blue
  blue
  blue
  green
  blue
  blue
  blue
  blue
  blue
  ...
  ```

+ To see the exact split, tally the results instead of eyeballing them.

  ```
  for i in $(seq 1 50); do curl -s http://localhost:30090; done | sort | uniq -c
  ```

  ```
      40 blue
      10 green
  ```

+ You can also tail the logs of the canary pod specifically, to correlate which requests it served.

  ```
  kubectl logs -l app=colorapp,version=v2 -f
  ```

## Step 6: Promote or rollback the canary

Once you're confident <code style="color : red">v2</code> (green) is behaving correctly, there are two ways forward:

+ **Promote**, by shifting more replicas towards <code style="color : red">v2</code> and away from <code style="color : red">v1</code>, until <code style="color : red">v1</code> reaches zero.

  ```
  kubectl scale deploy colorapp-v2 --replicas=4
  kubectl scale deploy colorapp-v1 --replicas=1

  # once satisfied, retire v1 completely
  kubectl scale deploy colorapp-v1 --replicas=0
  ```

+ **Rollback**, by simply scaling the canary back down to zero, or deleting it outright. Since <code style="color : red">v1</code> never stopped serving traffic, this is instant and safe.

  ```
  kubectl scale deploy colorapp-v2 --replicas=0
  # or
  kubectl delete -f v2-deployment.yaml
  ```

## Conclusion

This section describes on a high-level the difference between the two deployment strategies used above.

An **existing (stable) deployment**, <code style="color : red">v1</code>, is the version already trusted to serve 100% of production traffic. A **canary deployment**, <code style="color : red">v2</code>, is a small, controlled slice of the same application, released alongside <code style="color : red">v1</code> to validate a change before committing to it fully.

Both deployments share the same <code style="color : red">app: colorapp</code> label, which is the only thing the Service's selector cares about. The <code style="color : red">version</code> label exists purely so we, as operators, can target, count, and scale each version independently with <code style="color : red">kubectl</code>. The traffic split itself is not weighted by any smart routing rule, it's simply proportional to how many pods of each version exist behind the Service.

This replica-ratio approach is intentionally low-tech, no Ingress controller, no service mesh, just <code style="color : red">Deployment</code>, <code style="color : red">Service</code>, and <code style="color : red">kubectl scale</code>. It's a good fit for a small self-managed cluster like mine. For finer-grained control, like an exact 5% canary regardless of replica count, or routing based on headers/cookies, a proper Ingress controller or service mesh with weighted routing would be the next step, and something I plan to explore in a future article.
